window.docContents[34].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-copy-counts-and-isomorphisms":{"contents":"Mathlib's copyCount counts the unlabelled copies of H in G as the subgraphs of\nG that are isomorphic to H, and labelledCopyCount counts the labelled copies.\nNote the argument order: Copy A B lists the copied graph first, like\nA →g B, while B.labelledCopyCount A and B.copyCount A are invariants of the\nhost graph B, which comes first.\n\n#source copyCount         -- #{G' : G.Subgraph | Nonempty (H ≃g G'.coe)}\n#source labelledCopyCount -- Fintype.card (Copy H G)\n\n\nIsomorphisms A ≃g A' and B ≃g B' identify the copies of A in B with the\ncopies of A' in B', by composing on both sides. So isomorphic graphs have the\nsame copy counts. This lets us count copies in whichever model of a graph is most\nconvenient.\n\nIsomorphisms A ≃g A' and B ≃g B' identify the copies of A in B with the\ncopies of A' in B'./-- Isomorphisms A ≃g A' and B ≃g B' identify the copies of A in B with the\ncopies of A' in B'. -/\ndef Iso.copyCongr {V' W' : Type*} {A : SimpleGraph V} {A' : SimpleGraph V'}\n    {B : SimpleGraph W} {B' : SimpleGraph W'} (eA : A ≃g A') (eB : B ≃g B') :\n    Copy A B ≃ Copy A' B' where\n  toFun f := (eB.toCopy.comp f).comp eA.symm.toCopy\n  invFun f := (eB.symm.toCopy.comp f).comp eA.toCopy\n  left_inv f := by ext; simp\n  right_inv f := by ext; simp\n\n\nlemma Iso.labelledCopyCount_eq {V' W' : Type*} [Fintype V] [Fintype V'] [Fintype W]\n    [Fintype W'] {A : SimpleGraph V} {A' : SimpleGraph V'} {B : SimpleGraph W}\n    {B' : SimpleGraph W'} (eA : A ≃g A') (eB : B ≃g B') :\n    B.labelledCopyCount A = B'.labelledCopyCount A' := by\n  simp only [labelledCopyCount, Fintype.card_eq_nat_card]\n  exact Nat.card_congr (eA.copyCongr eB)\n\n\nFor unlabelled copies only the host graphs need to be finite, so the next lemma\nassumes nothing about A and A'. Mathlib's labelledCopyCount also asks for the\ncopied graph to be finite, because it is defined as Fintype.card (Copy A B), and\nthe Fintype instance on Copy A B is built from the one on homomorphisms A →g B.\nMathematically we lose nothing: if B is finite and A is infinite then there are\nno copies at all.\n\nThe proof sends each subgraph of B to its image in B', and Copy.isoSubgraphMap\ncarries a labelling of a subgraph over to its image.\n\nlemma Iso.copyCount_eq {V' W' : Type*} [Fintype W] [Fintype W'] {A : SimpleGraph V}\n    {A' : SimpleGraph V'} {B : SimpleGraph W} {B' : SimpleGraph W'} (eA : A ≃g A')\n    (eB : B ≃g B') : B.copyCount A = B'.copyCount A' := by\n  classical\n  unfold copyCount\n  refine Finset.card_nbij' (Subgraph.map eB.toHom) (Subgraph.map eB.symm.toHom)\n    (fun C hC => ?_) (fun C hC => ?_) (fun C _ => ?_) (fun C _ => ?_)\n  · obtain ⟨f⟩ := (Finset.mem_filter.1 hC).2\n    exact Finset.mem_filter.2\n      ⟨Finset.mem_univ _, ⟨(eA.symm.trans f).trans (eB.toCopy.isoSubgraphMap C)⟩⟩\n  · obtain ⟨f⟩ := (Finset.mem_filter.1 hC).2\n    exact Finset.mem_filter.2\n      ⟨Finset.mem_univ _, ⟨(eA.trans f).trans (eB.symm.toCopy.isoSubgraphMap C)⟩⟩\n  -- mapping along eB and then back along eB.symm gives the original subgraph\n  · rw [← Subgraph.map_comp]; ext <;> simp\n  · rw [← Subgraph.map_comp]; ext <;> simp\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009Counting Copies*","header":"12.7.1. Copy Counts and Isomorphisms","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-copy-counts-and-isomorphisms"},"/Distance/#section-girth-of-the-mycielskian":{"contents":"The Mycielskian of a triangle-free graph is triangle-free, so its girth is at least\n4. Whether it is exactly 4 depends on whether G has two edges sharing an\nendpoint: if it does, the two edges give a 4-cycle through the apex; if not, the\nedges of G form a matching, and then the girth of mμ(G) is 5 provided G has\nan edge. (If G has no edges then mμ(G) is a star centred at the apex plus\nisolated vertices, so it is acyclic.)\n\nThe edges of G form a matching: no two edges share an endpoint, that is,\nevery vertex has at most one neighbour./-- The edges of G form a matching: no two edges share an endpoint, that is,\nevery vertex has at most one neighbour. -/\nabbrev EdgesFormMatching (G : SimpleGraph V) : Prop :=\n  ∀ x y z : V, G.Adj x y → G.Adj x z → y = z\n\n\nA pair of edges with a common endpoint, u — v and u — w, gives rise to a walk\nin the Mycielskian, none — vʳ — uˡ — wʳ — none. (If v ≠ w this is a\n4-cycle.)/-- A pair of edges with a common endpoint, u — v and u — w, gives rise to a walk\nin the Mycielskian, none — vʳ — uˡ — wʳ — none. (If v ≠ w this is a\n4-cycle.) -/\ndef mycielskianFourCycle {u v w : V} (h1 : G.Adj u v) (h2 : G.Adj u w) :\n    mμ(G).Walk none none :=\n  cons (by simp : mμ(G).Adj none vʳ) <| cons (by simp [h1.symm])\n    <| cons (by simpa : mμ(G).Adj uˡ wʳ) <| cons (by simp) nil\n\n\nlemma isCycle_mycielskianFourCycle {u v w : V} (h1 : G.Adj u v) (h2 : G.Adj u w)\n    (h : v ≠ w) : (mycielskianFourCycle h1 h2).IsCycle := by\n  simp [mycielskianFourCycle, cons_isCycle_iff, h]\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma length_mycielskianFourCycle {u v w : V} (h1 : G.Adj u v) (h2 : G.Adj u w) :\n    (mycielskianFourCycle h1 h2).length = 4 := rfl\n\n\nIf G is triangle-free and its edges do not form a matching, then mμ(G) has\ngirth 4.\n\nlemma egirth_mycielskian_eq_four (h3 : G.CliqueFree 3)\n    (hnm : ¬G.EdgesFormMatching) : egirth mμ(G) = 4 := by\n  obtain ⟨x, y, z, h1, h2, hvw⟩ : ∃ x y z, G.Adj x y ∧ G.Adj x z ∧ y ≠ z := by grind\n  refine (four_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three h3.mycielskian).antisymm' ?_\n  simpa using (isCycle_mycielskianFourCycle h1 h2 hvw).egirth_le_length\n\n\nIf the edges of G form a matching then mμ(G) is C[4].Free. We use\ncycleGraph_four_free_iff_common_neighbor_unique: given distinct vertices x and\ny with common neighbours u and v, we split into cases according to whether each\nof x, y, u and v is the apex, a left copy or a right copy. The adjacency\nrules of the Mycielskian dispose of most of the 81 cases, and grind finishes the\nrest.\n\nlemma cycleGraph_four_free_mycielskian (hmatch : G.EdgesFormMatching) :\n    C[4].Free mμ(G) := by\n  apply cycleGraph_four_free_iff_common_neighbor_unique.mpr\n  -- each of x, y, u and v is the apex, a left copy or a right copy: 81 cases\n  rintro (_ | (x | x)) (_ | (y | y)) (_ | (u | u)) (_ | (v | v)) h1 h2 h3 h4 hxy <;>\n    simp_all only [mycielskian_adj] <;> -- 12 goals remain\n    grind [Adj.symm]\n\n\nA graph in which no two edges share a common endpoint is triangle-free.\n\nlemma EdgesFormMatching.cliqueFree_three (hmatch : G.EdgesFormMatching) :\n    G.CliqueFree 3 := fun t ht => by\n  classical\n  obtain ⟨a, b, c, hab, hac, hbc, -⟩ := is3Clique_iff.1 ht\n  exact hbc.ne (hmatch a b c hab hac)\n\n\nIf G has an edge and its edges form a matching, then mμ(G) has girth 5.\n\nlemma egirth_mycielskian_eq_five {u v : V} (hadj : G.Adj u v)\n    (hmatch : G.EdgesFormMatching) : egirth mμ(G) = 5 := by\n  refine le_antisymm ?_ (five_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three_of_free\n    hmatch.cliqueFree_three.mycielskian (cycleGraph_four_free_mycielskian hmatch))\n  simpa [mycielskianClosed] using\n    (isCycle_mycielskianClosed_of_adj hadj).egirth_le_length\n\n\nThe 5-cycle of isCycle_mycielskianClosed_of_adj exists for every edge of G, so\nthe Mycielskian of any graph with an edge has girth at most 5, however large the\ngirth of G. So Mycielski's construction gives triangle-free graphs of large\nchromatic number, but not graphs of large girth and large chromatic number. That such\ngraphs exist is a famous theorem of Erdős P. Erdős, Graph theory and probability, Canad. J. Math. 11 (1959), 34–38., proved by the\nprobabilistic method.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Girth","header":"11.4.5. Girth of the Mycielskian","id":"/Distance/#section-girth-of-the-mycielskian"}});